#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Makes Navigation's terrain overview, its zones' outlines and its stitched map from the game's own files.

Every map the set Tiles has a grid for is drawn alike: the two continents and Zephras Isle, a zone straight on
the world map with a grid of its own (its own instance, 2991; Navigation's Coords.Planes). Below, a continent
is any of them.

Up close the map draws the game's minimap textures themselves, one of 512 x 512 per tile of a continent's
grid (Core's set Tiles names the file of each). Far out that would be hundreds of large textures at once,
so there the map draws this overview instead: the same tiles at 64 x 64 texels each, eight by eight in one
texture of 512 x 512, so a whole continent is a few dozen textures (src/Navigation/Terrain.lua).

The water is cut away: each texel's alpha is how much of it is land, read from the game's terrain files,
where the ground lies above the water's surface (its heights and the water of each map chunk; magma and
slime stay), fading out in the first yard under it, so a beach one wades on stays. What stands on the water
stays too (a dock, a pier, a bridge, a ship at its mooring: the ground under it is the sea's floor): where the
minimap shows something brighter or warmer than the water around it. So is the open sky around an isle that floats (Zephras Isle), where there is no water: what the
game's minimap textures leave pure black (it draws nothing there: the terrain's holes, the cliffs under the
rim), as far as it reaches out to the edge of the map's tiles; a dark place within the land and a building
over the sky (a dock) stay. Nothing is land along a tile's side that faces no tile, the world's rim (RIM map
chunks deep): the game leaves the sea floor there unsculpted and dry at the water's level, or its minimap a grey
band, which showed as lines in the open sea. The land's colour is taken from land alone, so no dark water rims a coast;
transparent texels carry the nearest land's colour, so filtering never draws a dark edge either. Up close the same
alpha is the mask of the game's own textures (Terrain.lua), so the coast is cut alike at every zoom; a tile on a
coast has a mask of its own, at four times the overview's detail, so a pier a few yards wide is not lost.

  assets/optimized/Navigation/Art/Terrain/<continent>-<row>-<column>.blp   row and column of the block of 8 x 8 tiles
                                                              (0 to 7), only blocks that hold a tile
  assets/optimized/Navigation/Art/Coasts/<key>.blp 128 x 128, a tile on a coast (its key in the set Tiles)
  src/Navigation/Coasts.lua                                    the tiles that have one, by continent (ns.COASTS)

Each zone's outline, for Blizzard's map art cut to its zone's shape (src/Navigation/Art.lua; a zone of a
continent's map, or one straight on the world map, as the isle): the map chunks
(a 16th of a tile, 33 1/3 yards) of the zone's areas, as the alpha of a mask over the zone map's box (the
game's world position of its corners); of the open sea only what lies near its land, so a zone's painted sea
does not reach out to its box's edge (a lake or a river is its zone's). Whole up to the border, it fades out past it into the neighbour (some
150 yards), and off the paper frame at the box's edges: two zones that meet overlap there, and the one drawn
over the other fades into it, so no line shows between them.

  assets/optimized/Navigation/Art/Zones/<uiMapID>.blp 256 x 256, over the zone map's box
  src/Navigation/Outlines.lua                  the zones that have one, by continent (ns.OUTLINES)

The stitched map, Navigation's own map of each continent made of Blizzard's zone maps (the view Sketches
far out, and the overlay): every zone's full map (its art and every part the game reveals once explored),
cut by its outline, laid where its box is on the continent's grid, at the overview's size; see-through where
no zone is (Art.lua draws Blizzard's continent and world maps under it). And the mask that fades their paper
frames off.

  assets/optimized/Navigation/Art/Sketches/<continent>-<row>-<column>.blp   as the terrain's overview
  assets/optimized/Navigation/Art/Edge.blp 256 x 256: a map's box, its frame faded off

The sea's waves, laid over the sea's colour wherever no land is (src/Navigation/Canvas.lua): the game's own ocean
texture, the first frame of the ones it lays over its seas (XTextures/ocean/ocean_h.1.blp, file ID 219855), its
alpha alone in white, so the code tints the light on the waves and fades it. Made a quarter of its size, for small
waves (a copy is drawn 91 units wide: the texture's own size on a 4K screen); made smaller as a tile of a larger
plane, so it still tiles seamlessly, as in the game. Made by itself, from that one file (--sea).

  assets/optimized/Navigation/Art/Sea.blp 256 x 256: the waves' light
  assets/optimized/Navigation/Art/SeaShade.blp 256 x 256: the sea's shade, slow patches of darker water drawn over
                                             the waves, so their copies do not show as tiles (Saneroki, 2026-11-11:
                                             "it is very tiled"): a tileable noise (white noise softened in the
                                             frequency domain, so it repeats seamlessly by itself), alpha only

The Maelstrom's whirlpool, laid over the sea where the game's world map paints it (src/Navigation/Canvas.lua,
Canvas.WHIRLPOOL; Saneroki, 2026-11-15: "add a whirlpool into the middle of the sea like it is on the original
maps"): the spiral of WoW: Forever's world map art, its ink alone (how much darker and redder than the parchment
under it) and the parchment's light at its middle, faded out round a circle, its alpha in white, so the code tints
it as the waves' foam. Made by itself, from the client tables and the map art (--whirlpool); it prints the numbers
Canvas.WHIRLPOOL places it by.

  assets/optimized/Navigation/Art/Whirlpool.blp 256 x 256: the spiral's ink and light

All are BLP2 with DXT5 (the alpha needs its own block) and a full chain of mipmaps; the DXT blocks are made
by ImageMagick (its range fit).

The input, all as the game stores them, for every tile the master of the set Tiles names
(assets/data/Tiles/Tiles.jsonl):
  minimap textures   a folder of the game's minimap textures, each named <file ID>.blp
  terrain files      a folder of the game's terrain files (root ADT) of the continents, each named
                     <key>.adt by the tile's key in the set Tiles
  client tables      a folder of the game's client tables as CSV, one a table (LiquidType, LiquidMaterial,
                     AreaTable, UiMap, UiMapAssignment, UiMapXMapArt, UiMapArtTile, WorldMapOverlay,
                     WorldMapOverlayTile)
  map art            a folder of the game's map textures (every map's art and every part revealed once
                     explored), each named <file ID>.blp
Run it again when the set or the game's build changes. With --maps (instances, as 2991) only those maps are
drawn: the other maps' textures, outlines and lines of Outlines.lua stay as they are.

Needs Python with Pillow and NumPy, and ImageMagick 7 (magick).

Usage:  dev/tools/terrain-art <minimap textures> <terrain files> <client tables> <map art> [--maps <id>[,<id>...]]
        dev/tools/terrain-art --sea <the game's ocean texture, as it stores it>
        dev/tools/terrain-art --whirlpool <client tables> <map art>
"""
